            {
              "id": "bffefa27-bea2-389a-a865-f8922e26855b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard library function in C to convert a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors well. If the input string is not a valid integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use safer alternatives like `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(argv[argIdx++])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[argIdx++], &end, 10);\nif (errno == ERANGE || *end != '\\0' || end == argv[argIdx]) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
